Tests for ALB_JACK_BEAT_TRACKING (JackSync beats counted through tempo
changes; see ../beat_tracker.hpp).

Unit tests, no JACK needed::

    make check

Integration test: the real engine against a scripted, Hydrogen-like JACK
timebase master on a private dummy JACK server (no hardware, no effect on
a running rig; the engine's HOME is a temporary directory).  Needs jackd,
oscsend (liblo), python3 with the ``jack`` module, and an engine built
with ALB_SYNC_PULSE_LOG defined, since it reads the pulses from that log::

    ./jack_integration_test.py                   # built with ALB_JACK_BEAT_TRACKING
    ./jack_integration_test.py --expect stock    # built without it

The first passes when every sync pulse lands within 2 samples of the
master's beat through tempo ramps, a relocate while rolling, a stop/start
without a locate, and a stop / locate 0 / start.  The second passes when
the stock code's drift is reproduced.  About 40 s each.  Set SL_BT_KEEP=1
to keep the run's files for inspection.

Hydrogen missed-cycle test: real Hydrogen as timebase master, the real
engine, rolling tempo changes, then Hydrogen frozen for 30 ms so it misses
cycles.  Hydrogen 1.2.4-1.2.7 drop their tempo offset when that happens,
while the engine keeps it, so the loops end up off the drums until the
next relocate.  Same isolation as above (private dummy JACK server, own
HOMEs, private OSC ports); also needs numpy and a Hydrogen AppImage or
build tree::

    ./hydrogen_missed_cycle_test.py --appimage /path/to/Hydrogen-1.2.4-x86_64.AppImage
    ./hydrogen_missed_cycle_test.py --h2-root /path/to/tree --expect kept

The first passes when the drift is reproduced (about 0.4 beat after the
freeze), the second when a fixed Hydrogen stays on the beat.  About 45 s.

``--pattern-mode`` runs Hydrogen in pattern mode, as on the rig, instead
of song mode.  ``--restart-engine`` also restarts the engine while rolling,
after the freeze, and adds a window D after it: with ALB_JACK_BBT_TRACKING
and a Hydrogen that publishes its real position in pattern mode (patched,
see ~/programming/c_and_cpp/hydrogen/ROADMAP.rst), D is on the beat; an
engine that does not follow comes back about 0.4 beat off.  With
``--expect reset`` D is not judged.

``--realign`` sends the engine's realign (``/set realign_transport 1``)
after the freeze, or after the restart with ``--restart-engine``; window E
after it must be on the beat whatever Hydrogen did, and the run also
reports the engine's pulse spacing around it (must stay one beat in wall
time: the loops' grid does not move), the kick intervals (the drums'
correction shows as one interval off a beat) and what the transport did
(JACK holds it for one period on a relocate).  Since the engine realigns
by itself when it starts with the transport rolling, ``--restart-engine``
alone also expects D on the beat with a stock Hydrogen that kept its
offset (``--freeze-ms 0``).  The analysis works in wall-clock JACK frames,
so relocations, the realign's backwards ones among them, do not confuse
it.  With ``--realign`` it also lists every relocation with whether the
engine reset the loops' sync counters there (the pulse log's
``event=sync_reset``): the realign's must keep them, even when it moves
the transport backwards, which a tempo rise and a Hydrogen reset make it
do; the final relocate to frame 0 resets them, as stock does.

``--toggle`` ends with Hydrogen's Play/Stop toggle pressed twice (locate
to the start and stop, then play) instead of a JACK relocate to frame 0,
the way the rig's transport button resets the drums; window C must still
be on the beat.

The 30 ms freeze does not always make Hydrogen miss cycles on a busy
machine, so a stock Hydrogen sometimes keeps its offset and an
``--expect reset`` run fails for that reason alone; repeat it.

Hydrogen take test: the same setup, but the engine records actual takes.
A probe client plays exactly with the drums (copies them into the engine
from a downbeat for 8 beats, pressing record at the start and end), with
one bar per cycle and Sync switched off and on at transport stops and
starts as the rig's big_tempo does::

    ./hydrogen_take_test.py --h2-root DIR

Take A comes after a tempo change while rolling, take B after Hydrogen
has dropped its offset (the engine's bar lines off the drums), take C
after Hydrogen's Play/Stop toggle pressed twice.  It passes when A and C
come out 8 beats long, with all 8 hits per pass, in time with the drums.
B is reported only: with the bar lines off, the loop is still the right
length and in time, but its record window is shifted, so part of the
phrase is missing.  A run where the dummy server skips cycles during a
take is INCONCLUSIVE (exit status 2): other jobs at normal priority starve
it under nice 19.  About 90 s.

``--auto-realign`` (Hydrogen missed-cycle test) turns on the engine's
``auto_realign``: the tempo change while rolling is followed by an
automatic realign, so the freeze finds no offset to drop and window B must
be on the beat whatever ``--expect`` says; the automatic relocation must
keep the loops' sync counters.  ``--slow-sync N`` makes the probe a
slow-sync client, not ready for N cycles each time the transport is
starting; with ``--auto-realign`` the engine must measure the longer hold,
stop auto-realigning, say so on its console and return 2 for ``/get auto_realign``.
